% PROJECT ABSTRACT
\sectiontitle{Project Abstract (2 pts)}

\vspace{0.02cm}
\vspace{0.15cm}

\answerboxauto{CheckMate is an interactive chess application developed to show the practical utility of foundational Data Structures and Algorithms in a complete desktop application. Rather than using pre-packaged libraries such as the C++ Standard Template Library (STL), all core structures are written manually in C using direct pointers and explicit dynamic memory management. The application uses an 8x8 two-dimensional array for instant board access, a pointer-based stack for LIFO move undos, a doubly linked list for chronological game history logging, a FIFO queue for buffering move commands, and an FNV-1a hash table for board repetition detection. A single-player AI opponent operates using minimax evaluation with alpha-beta cutoffs. To meet our mentor's requirements, a 3D graphical interface built with Raylib is linked directly over this engine to support visual board interaction and mouse-based move input for our Phase-II evaluation.}

\vspace{0.55cm}

% UPDATED PROJECT APPROACH
\sectiontitle{Updated Project Approach and Architecture (2 pts)}

\vspace{0.02cm}
\vspace{0.15cm}

\answerboxauto{The project follows a modular three-tier structure cleanly separating manual C data structures from the C++ game engine and the graphical presentation layer:
\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}\setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
    \item \textbf{Data Structure Core (Pure C):} Modules include \texttt{undo\_stack.c} (LIFO stack rollback), \texttt{move\_history.c} (doubly linked list move log), \texttt{event\_queue.c} (FIFO command buffering), and \texttt{board\_hash.c} (FNV-1a hash table for threefold repetition tracking).
    \item \textbf{Game Rules \& AI Layer (C++):} \texttt{chess\_rules.cpp} controls directional ray casting, check/checkmate detection, and legal move filtering; \texttt{minimax.cpp} handles tree search with material scoring and MVV-LVA move ordering.
    \item \textbf{Presentation Layer (Raylib):} A dedicated graphical module (\texttt{gui\_3d.cpp}) displays the 8x8 board in 3D perspective, handles mouse ray-plane intersection to translate screen clicks into board tiles, and displays on-screen DSA telemetry.
\end{itemize}
All modules run locally on the desktop without network protocols or external web APIs, eliminating external communication latency and third-party dependencies.}

